you are watching a clip from the john  perry channel  genetics and evolution  have you focused much on the the origin  of signaling i know stuart west who well  i wrote two papers jointly with john  krebs  about  signaling and  the burden of those really was to  refute the then fashionable idea among  ethologists that animal communication  is all about  cooperative transfer of information  so the assumption was made by people  like mile maestro nico tinbergen and and  others of his his colleagues  was that  social life needs kind of oiling  the wheels need oiling by  smooth running communication and the the  signaler  needs to be understood by the receiver  of the signal and and vice versa what  krebs and i substituted for that was a  more manipulative approach we  we thought that  um the selfish gene where looking at  life  was not really compatible with this  highly cooperative vision  of animal signals  and so um when  something like a um a male bird sings  and uh  this causes  the ovaries of the female to grow which  it does that's known physiologically  it's been shown in doves and in canaries  we saw krebs and i saw it as a  manipulation  we made the analogy with  uh the  human experimenter who by injecting  hormones into the female bird could  cause her ovaries to grow  or  a physiologist who sticks electrodes  into the brain  of an animal and makes it behave rather  like a puppeteer pulling strings  we saw the influence of one animal on  another via signals as equivalent to  that  so  the the male bird is trying to influence  the behavior of the female bird or the  or um  of another male perhaps to intimidate  if the male bird was a physiologist  capable of in of injecting hormones or  capable of  sticking  electrodes into the brain of the rival  or the  prospective mate then he would do so but  he can't do that what he can do  is enter the brain of the victim  use the word advisedly  by the portholes which already exist  which are the eyes and ears and nose etc  and that's what he does  and so animal signals crabs an eyesore  as  manipulation  direct influences  on the physiology of the  uh  victim animal and i tie this up with my  idea of the extended phenotype which is  another book that i wrote  where the behavior of the victim can be  thought of as part of the phenotypic  expression  of genes in the manipulator  and the cuckoo is a classic example of  this where  the cuckoo is a parasite but unlike  parasites which live inside the body of  the host  where parasitologists have already  established uh very strong evidence for  physiological direct physiological  evidence of the parasite on the host i  would call  extended phenotypic expression of the  genes of the parasite in the phenotype  of the host in the case of the cuckoo  the parasite is not living inside the  host  the parasite is sitting outside the host  but nevertheless exerting just the same  kind of influence  as a parasite of say a fluke say or a  tapeworm  being inside the host this is direct  manipulation  and krebs and i generalized this  to the notion that all animal signals  are  manipulative in the same kind of way  as the cuckoo manipulating  its host we call i i call that action at  a distance  well the um  would it would always be considered  manipulative if it's helping both  individuals because there are a bunch of  communications that happen that help  both individuals exactly yeah yeah  i could say that the mating call is one  like the female wants to reproduce as  well as yeah it's  both of their best interests right  yeah good good point and in those cases  where it is in both of their best  interests  then the point we made was that rather  than shouting which is what much of  animal signals is about  either literally auditory shouting or  visual shouting bright colors loud  voices and so on you don't need that  if the victim is no longer a victim but  is actually a co-operator it actually  wants to be manipulated so to speak  then the signal ceases to be a shout and  becomes what we called a conspiratorial  whisper  and so you tend to get a decrease  in um  in intensity of the signal in those  cases where the signal  increases in those cases where the  signal gets louder and louder over  evolutionary time or brighter and  brighter over evolutionary time  then our interpretation would be that  that is a is a  manifestation of resistance on the part  of the  signal lee on the part of the victim so  where the victim is resisting being  manipulated you expect the signal to  become louder and louder  and where the victim is  cooperating wants to be manipulated in  other words the world manipulation isn't  really even appropriate where that  happens then the signal drops in volume  and becomes a conspiratorial whisper  so  so quiet that maybe the experiment maybe  the ethologist scarcely even notices it  so that was just a clip from my  conversation with richard dawkins you  can see the whole thing there's a link  to that down below subscribe to this  channel if you enjoyed it  i upload clips pretty regularly talks  and conversations and presentations that  i've done on genetics and evolution and  i am gearing up to start doing weekly  videos again here that are completely  new completely fresh
 

